{
  "correction_rules": [
    "General bias: model is slightly low in rising regimes (true outcomes run ~+0.3 to +1.5 above forecast for the first ~5–15 days when trend is up). Apply a small upward bias when the last 5–10 days show positive momentum.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: beyond ~day 15–20 the model tends to revert too hard / drift downward relative to reality (notably Examples 3–5 where forecasts fall into low 60s while true stays mid/high 60s). Damp long-horizon mean reversion; anchor to a slower-moving level (e.g., 20–30d average).",
    "Overshoot after local highs: when the model projects a continued rally to new highs (e.g., forecasts near 70), realized prices often peak lower and roll over sooner. Cap upside extensions: reduce forecast increments once projected price is >~2–3 above recent 10–20d mean.",
    "Underestimation of short-term volatility spikes: on strong up-move days the model underpredicts the magnitude of the next 1–10 day jump. Add a momentum boost for short horizons when recent returns are large and positive.",
    "Timing/lag: turning points are predicted early/late depending on regime—model often calls the downturn too early at long horizons (Examples 3–4). Introduce lag to regime-switching: require confirmation (multiple down days) before projecting sustained declines."
  ]
}
